<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout component="day23" xml:id="day-23-lecture">
  <title>Day 23</title>

  <introduction>

    <p>
        8.2.1 Bagging
    </p>
    <p>
        8.2.2 Random forest
    </p>
  </introduction>

<objectives>
  <ul>
    <li><p>Explain what bagging of decision trees accomplishes.</p></li>
    <li>
      <p>Describe how to use out-of-bag error estimation for decision trees.
      <ul>
        <li><p>Describe the procedure for both regression and classification trees.</p></li>
      </ul>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li><p>Explain what problem of bagging is addressed by random forests.</p></li>
    <li><p>Describe the relationship between random forests and bagging.</p></li>
  </ul>
</objectives>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Bagging: Use ensemble of trees to reduce variance</title>
  <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
    <stack>
      <p>Want to do (but can't):</p>
      <p>Build separate models from independent training sets, and average resulting predictions:</p>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p><m>\hat f^1(x), \cdots, \hat f^B(x)</m> for <m>B</m> separate training sets.</p></li>
          <li><p>Return the average:</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>
          \hat f_{\text{avg}}(x) = \frac{1}{B} \sum_{b=1}^B \hat f^b(x)
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <ul >
          <li><p>Decreases variance, but is not practical.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
    <stack>
      <p>Bootstrap modification:</p>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Work with a fixed data set.</p></li>
          <li><p>Take <m>B</m> samples from this data set (with replacement).</p></li>
          <li><p>Train the method on the <m>b</m>th sample to get <m>\hat f^{*b}(x)</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Return the average of predictions (regression):</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>
          \hat f_{\text{bag}}(x) = \frac{1}{B} \sum_{b=1}^B \hat f^{*b}(x)
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>Or use majority vote (classification).</p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Tree version</title>
  <image source="figures/TreeBaggingCartoon.png" width="60%">
    <shortdescription>Visual representation of bagging showing a blue data block leading to multiple red sample blocks and green trees.</shortdescription>
  </image>
  <p>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Blue represents the original data set <m>X:n \times p</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Red represents the subsampled data sets.</p></li>
      <li><p>Build a tree from each subsampled data set.</p></li>
      <li><p>Obtain a prediction from each tree and return the average.</p></li>
        <li>
          <p>
            For classification, return the majority vote of the predictions from each tree.
          </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
</paragraphs>


<paragraphs>
  <title>Prediction on new data point</title>
  <image source="figures/TreeBaggingCartoon-OnlyTrees.png" width="60%">
    <shortdescription>Cartoon illustration of multiple green trees, representing an ensemble of decision trees.</shortdescription>
  </image>
  <p>
    <ul>
      <li><p>A new data point is passed through each tree, and the resulting predictions are averaged.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </p>
</paragraphs>

<example xml:id="regression_prediction_ex1">
  <title>Regression bagging example</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      For the shown data and bagged trees, predict the response for the new data point <m>x_0</m> (the yellow star).
    </p>
    <table>
    <title>Sample Data</title>
    <tabular halign="center">
        <row header="yes" bottom="major">
            <cell>Data Point</cell>
            <cell>Y</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>1</cell>
            <cell>4.2</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>2</cell>
            <cell>6.8</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>3</cell>
            <cell>5.1</cell>
        </row>
    </tabular>
</table>
    <image source="figures/regression_prediction_ex1.png">
      <shortdescription>A data set with 3 points, a new data point (yellow star) and three different baggings obtained by bootstrap sampling.</shortdescription>      
    </image>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      The predicted response for the new data point <m>x_0</m> is the average of the predictions from each bagged tree.
<md>    
        <mrow>\hat{f}_{\text{bag}}(x) \amp=\frac{1}{B}\sum_{b=1}^{B}\hat{f}^{*b}(x)</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{3}(6.8 + 4.2+6.8)= 5.93 </mrow>
</md>
      
    </p>
    <image source="figures/regression_prediction_ex1_answer.png">
      <shortdescription>A data set with 3 points, a new data point (yellow star) and three different baggings obtained by bootstrap sampling. Average prediction from all bagged trees is shown.</shortdescription>      
    </image>
  </solution>
</example>

<example>
  <title>Classification bagging example</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      For the shown data and bagged trees, predict the class (triangle or circle) for the new data point <m>x_0</m> (the yellow star).
    </p>
    <image source="figures/classification_prediction_ex1.png" xml:id="classification_prediction_ex1">
      <shortdescription>A data set with 3 points and two classes (triangles and circles), a new data point (yellow star) and three different baggings obtained by bootstrap sampling.</shortdescription>      
    </image>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      The predicted response for the new data point <m>x_0</m> is the majority vote from all the bagged trees, which is the circle class (2 out of 3 trees predict circle).
    </p>
    <image source="figures/classification_prediction_ex1_answer.png">
      <shortdescription>A data set with 3 points and two classes (triangles and circles), a new data point (yellow star) and three different baggings obtained by bootstrap sampling. Majority vote for predictions from all bagged trees is shown.</shortdescription>      
    </image>
  </solution>
</example>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Example: Heart classification data</title>
  <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
    <image source="figures/Fig8-8.png">
      <shortdescription>Line plot comparing test and out-of-bag error for bagging and random forest models as the number of trees increases.</shortdescription>
    </image>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>The example is for classification.</p></li>
        <li><p>Ignore everything but the black lines.</p></li>
        <li><p>The dashed line is the test error from a single classification tree.</p></li>
        <li><p>The solid black line is the test error from the bagged version.</p></li>
        <li><p><m>B</m> only needs to be large enough for the error to stabilize; about 100 trees is sufficient.</p></li>
        <li><p>The number of trees is not a flexibility parameter.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Estimating the test error: Out of Bag (OOB) error</title>

    <image source="figures/TreeBaggingCartoon-MoreTrees.png" width="60%">
      <shortdescription>Diagram showing several red data samples, each corresponding to an individual green decision tree, representing a bagging ensemble process.</shortdescription>
    </image>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>On average, a bootstrap sample uses about 2/3 of the data.</p></li>
          <li><p>The remaining observations not used are called out-of-bag (OOB) observations.</p></li>
          <li><p>For each observation, run it through all the trees for which it was not used in training.</p></li>
          <li><p>Return the average (or majority vote) of those predictions as the test prediction. This relies on the law of large numbers.</p></li>
          <li><p>For B sufficiently large, OOB error is equivalent to leave-one-out cross-validation error.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                OOB error is a good estimate of test error, and is more computationally efficient that cross-validation especially for large datasets.
              </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
</paragraphs>

<example>
  <title>OOB for classification</title>  
  <statement>
    <p>    
      What is the OOB error for the data and bagged trees shown below?    
    </p>
<image source="figures/classification_OOB_error_ex1.png" width="90%">
  <shortdescription>Four horizontal panels. The top shows the original data which contains 3 points from two classes (triangles and circles) and the 3 bags obtained using bootstrap sampling. Each of teh subsequent horizontal panels shows one of the bags with space for the students to compare the predicted classes versus the true classes for each OOB observation.</shortdescription>
  
</image>
  </statement>
  <hint>
    <p>
      The OOB error is the proportion of misclassified observations when using the majority vote of the trees for which each observation was not included in the training set. In this case, we would determine the OOB predictions for each of the three data points and then calculate the overall misclassification rate.
    </p>
  </hint>
    <solution>
<image source="figures/classification_OOB_error_ex1_answer.png" width="100%">
  <shortdescription>Four horizontal panels. The top shows the original data and the 3 bags obtained using bootstrap sampling. Each of teh subsequent horizontal panels shows the OOB predictions for each data point.</shortdescription>  
</image>
<p>
  The OOB classification error is <m>\frac{1}{3} \approx 0.33</m> since one of the three observations is misclassified when using the OOB predictions.
</p>
      </solution>
  
</example>

<example>
  <title>OOB for regression.</title>  
  <statement>
    <p>
      What is the OOB error for the data and bagged trees in <xref ref="regression_prediction_ex1"/>? Use the following table to help you compute the OOB predictions and the squared differences between the true response and the predicted response for each observation.
    </p>

          <table>
    <title>Sample Data</title>
    <tabular halign="center">
        <row header="yes" bottom="major">
            <cell>Data Point <m>y_i</m></cell>
            <cell>Y</cell>
            <cell><m>\hat{f}^{*1}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\hat{f}^{*2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\hat{f}^{*3}</m></cell>
            <cell>OOB Prediction <m>\hat{y}_i</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(y_i-\hat{y}_i)^2</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>1</cell>
            <cell>4.2</cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>2</cell>
            <cell>6.8</cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>3</cell>
            <cell>5.1</cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
        </row>
    </tabular>
</table>

  </statement>  
    <hint>
    <p>      
        The OOB error is the average of the squared differences between the true response and the predicted response for each observation, using only the trees for which that observation was not included in the training set. In this case, we would calculate the OOB predictions for each of the three data points and then compute the mean squared error.      
    </p>
  </hint>
<solution>
      <table>
    <title>Sample Data</title>
    <tabular halign="center">
        <row header="yes" bottom="major">
            <cell>Data Point <m>y_i</m></cell>
            <cell>Y</cell>
            <cell><m>\hat{f}^{*1}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\hat{f}^{*2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\hat{f}^{*3}</m></cell>
            <cell>OOB Prediction <m>\hat{y}_i</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(y_i-\hat{y}_i)^2</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>1</cell>
            <cell>4.2</cell>
            <cell>5.1</cell>
            <cell>None</cell>
            <cell>None</cell>
            <cell>5.1</cell>
            <cell>(4.2-5.1)^2 = 0.81</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>2</cell>
            <cell>6.8</cell>
            <cell>None</cell>
            <cell>4.2</cell>
            <cell>None</cell>
            <cell>4.2</cell>
            <cell>(6.8-4.2)^2 = 6.76</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>3</cell>
            <cell>5.1</cell>
            <cell>None</cell>
            <cell>None</cell>
            <cell>4.2</cell>
            <cell>4.2</cell>
            <cell>(5.1-4.2)^2 = 0.81</cell>
        </row>
    </tabular>
</table>
  <p>
    The OOB regression error is 
    <md>
    <mrow> \text{OOB MSE} \amp= \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n (y_i - \hat{y}_i)^2 </mrow>
    <mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{3}((4.2-5.1)^2 + (6.8-5.1)^2 + (5.1-5.1)^2)</mrow>
    <mrow> \amp= \frac{1}{3}(0.81 + 2.89 + 0) = \frac{3.7}{3} \approx 1.23</mrow>
    </md>
  Note that <m>n</m> in the denominator is the number of valid observations, i.e., observations that are out of bag for at least one of the bags. For example, if one observations is never out of the bag, then you would replace <m>n</m> by <m>n-1</m>. If 13 observations are never out of bag, the denominator becomes <m>n-13</m>. Never out of bag observations usually occur if the number of trees <m>B</m> is small, so increasing the number of trees ensures that all observations are out of bag for at least one tree.
  </p>
</solution>
</example>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Estimating the test error: OOB error</title>
  <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
    <image source="figures/Fig8-8.png">
      <shortdescription>Line plot comparing test and out-of-bag (OOB) error for bagging and random forest models as the number of trees increases.</shortdescription>
    </image>
    <stack>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>The green line represents OOB error.</p></li>
          <li><p>Note that OOB error is averaged over approximately <m>B/3</m> predictions per observation.</p></li>
          <li><p>The book suggests that the much lower OOB error may simply be due to random chance.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>      
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Random forests: The idea</title>
  <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
    <stack>
      <p>Goal: decorrelate the bagged trees.</p>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>If there is a strong predictor, the first split of most trees will be the same.</p></li>
          <li><p>Most or all trees will be highly correlated.</p></li>
          <li><p>Averaging highly correlated quantities does not decrease variance as much as averaging uncorrelated quantities.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
    <stack>
      <p>The random forest fix:</p>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Each time a split is considered, use only a random subset of <m>m</m> of the predictors.</p></li>
          <li><p>Take a fresh sample of predictors at every split.</p></li>
          <li><p>Typically for classification, <m>m \approx \sqrt{p}</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>For regression, <m>m \approx p/3</m> is a common starting point (often tuned further using the OOB error). </p></li>
          <li><p>On average, <m>(p-m)/p</m> of the splits will not consider the strong predictor.</p></li>
          <li><p><m>m=p</m> gives back bagging.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<example>
  <title>Sketching random forest splits</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      In this example, we will practice sketching the splits made by a random forest model in a two-dimensional predictor space. The provided figure shows the predictor plane defined by <m>X_1</m> and <m>X_2</m>, along with dashed lines that represent potential split locations. 
    </p>
    <image source="figures/random_forests_sketching_ex1.png" width="60%">
      <shortdescription>A square showing the regions for two predictors X1 and X2 with dashed lines that define a non-uniform grid for answering the question.</shortdescription>
      </image>

<table xml:id="random_forest_splits_ex1">
  <title>Random number generator calls</title>
  <tabular>
    <row>
      <cell>Call</cell>
      <cell>Random number</cell>
      <cell>Call</cell>
      <cell>Random number</cell>
    </row>
    <row><cell><m>c_1</m>   </cell><cell>2</cell><cell><m>c_{17}</m></cell><cell>2</cell></row>
    <row><cell><m>c_2</m>   </cell><cell>1</cell><cell><m>c_{18}</m></cell><cell>1</cell></row>
    <row><cell><m>c_3</m>   </cell><cell>2</cell><cell><m>c_{19}</m></cell><cell>1</cell></row>
    <row><cell><m>c_4</m>   </cell><cell>1</cell><cell><m>c_{20}</m></cell><cell>2</cell></row>
    <row><cell><m>c_5</m>   </cell><cell>1</cell><cell><m>c_{21}</m></cell><cell>2</cell></row>
    <row><cell><m>c_6</m>   </cell><cell>1</cell><cell><m>c_{22}</m></cell><cell>1</cell></row>
    <row><cell><m>c_7</m>   </cell><cell>2</cell><cell><m>c_{23}</m></cell><cell>2</cell></row>
    <row><cell><m>c_8</m>   </cell><cell>2</cell><cell><m>c_{24}</m></cell><cell>1</cell></row>
    <row><cell><m>c_9</m>   </cell><cell>1</cell><cell><m>c_{25}</m></cell><cell>1</cell></row>
