  </tabular>
</table>
  </introduction>
  <task>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Using random forests with depth 4, sketch the splits in the provided predictor plane <m>(X_1,X_2)</m> using as many of the random numbers shown in <xref ref="random_forest_splits_ex1"/> for each split as needed. Align your splits with the dashed lines in the provided figure. your choice of which dashed line to choose for the split in each region is not important (as long as you are able to make all the required splits), but the split variable must be correct. Assume that no stopping criterion is reached, i.e., you need to make all possible splits for the given tree depth. 
    </p>
    <p>
      There can be multiple valid answers, but the number of regions should be the same for all of them.
    </p>
      </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        This is a possible solution.
      </p>
      <image source="figures/random_forests_sketching_ex1_answer.png" width="75%">
        <shortdescription>A square showing the regions for two predictors X1 and X2 with one possible splitting of the predictor space into a non-uniform grid.</shortdescription>
        
      </image>
    </answer>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            How many total regions did you end up with?
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            You should end up with <m>2^4=16</m> regions, since each split divides a region into two new regions, and the depth of the tree is 4.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </task>
</example>



<paragraphs>
  <title>Example on gene expression</title>
  <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
    <image source="figures/Fig8-10.png">
      <shortdescription>Plot of test classification error versus number of trees comparing random forests with m=p, m=p/2, and m=square root of p.</shortdescription>
    </image>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>Helpful for large <m>p</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p>Predict one of 14 types of cancer (15 total classes), using the 500 genes with the most variance.</p></li>
        <li><p>Randomly split the data into training and test sets, and compare three values of <m>m</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p>Error rate of a single tree: 45.7%.</p></li>
        <li><p>Null error rate: 75.4% (classify every observation into the dominant overall class, which here is the healthy class).</p></li>
        <li><p>400 trees are enough for good performance.</p></li>
        <li><p><m>m=\sqrt{p}</m> provides a small improvement over bagging (<m>m=p</m>) in this case.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>TL;DR</title>
  
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>Bagging: Trees are grown independently on random samples. The trees tend to be similar to each other, which can result in getting caught in local optima.</p></li>
        <li><p>Random forest: Trees are grown independently on samples, but each split is made using a random subset of features.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  
</paragraphs>

<exercise>
  <title>Computing random forest splits for classification</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      In this exercise, we will practice computing the splits made by a random forest model in a two-dimensional predictor space for a classification problem. The provided figure shows the predictor plane defined by <m>X_1</m> and <m>X_2</m>, the data points, and the dashed lines that represent potential split locations. 
    </p>

    <p>
  The <term>split error</term> for classification is the weighted average of the errors for the left and right nodes, where the weights are the proportions of observations in each node. The formula for the error <m>E_s</m> is given below for split <m>s</m>.
  <md>
  \text{E}_s = \frac{N_{\text{left}}}{N} \times E_{\text{left}} + \frac{N_{\text{right}}}{N} \times E_{\text{right}}
</md>
where <m>N</m> is the number of points in the parent node, <m>N_{\text{left}}</m> and <m>N_{\text{right}}</m> are the number of points in the left and right child nodes, respectively. <m>E_{\text{left}}</m> and <m>E_{\text{right}}</m> are the classification errors for the left and right child nodes, respectively.

</p>
<image source="figures/random_forests_splitting_ex1.png" width="60%">
  <shortdescription>Qualitative data (three circles and three triangles) on a two-dimensional predictor space, X1 and X2. A non-uniform grid of all potential split locations is shown as dashed lines.</shortdescription>  
</image>

  </introduction>
  <task>
    <statement>
     <p>
  Using recursive binary splitting with tree depth of 2, how many errors corresponding to possible splits will you need to compute?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        You will need to compute the errors for 4 rows in the table <xref ref="split_errors_ex1"/> to determine the first split.
      </p>
      <p>
        Next, you will need to compute 5 more errors for splitting one of the child nodes along <m>X_1</m>, and at most 3 more errors for splitting the other child node along <m>X_2</m>. So altogether, you will need to compute at most 12 errors to determine the first and second splits. This is because each split divides the data into two child nodes, and you will need to evaluate the potential splits for each child node separately.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </task>
  <task>
  <statement>
    <p>
  Using recursive binary splitting, draw the splits corresponding to tree depth of 2 using as many rows of the randomly generated sequence <xref ref="random_forest_splits_ex1"/> as needed to decide the predictor to split on. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Table <xref ref="split_errors_ex1"/> provides a starting point for you, but you must append additional rows to the table for the additional splits. 
  </p>
<p>
   The split variable and split point for each split must be determined by the split that minimizes the classification error.
</p>

<table xml:id="split_errors_ex1">
    <title>Starting table for classification error rate versus splits. Add more rows as needed.</title>
    <tabular halign="center">
        <row header="yes" bottom="major">
            <cell>Splits</cell>
            <cell><m>E_{\text{left}}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>E_{\text{right}}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>E</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row><cell><m>s_{21}</m></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>s_{22}</m></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>s_{23}</m></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
